  "worked_solution": "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\u27e9 & =\u27e82t-1,3t\u27e9 \\\\  & =(2t-1){|}_{0}\\cdot (3t){|}_{0}+(2t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (3t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(2(0)-1)\\cdot (3(0))+(2(1)-1)\\cdot (3(1)) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)\u22a5\u0338{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\u27e9 & =\u27e83t,1-t\u27e9 \\\\  & =(3t){|}_{0}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{0}+(3t){|}_{1}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(3(0))\\cdot (1-0)+(3(1))\\cdot (1-1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)\u22a5{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{1}(t)\u22a5\u0338{p}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
